        text: "It would be reasonable to assume that in an era of webinars, on-demand CLE, and active online legal communities, in-person conferences have lost much of their relevance. In practice, the opposite has largely held true. Digital content has become abundant enough that the differentiating value of an in-person event now lies less in access to information, which is widely available online, and more in the quality of unstructured conversation, the ability to build genuine relationships over a few days rather than a single video call, and the accountability that comes from committing travel time and budget to a specific professional development goal. Firms that treat conference attendance purely as a content-consumption exercise are missing much of what actually makes these events worth attending.",
